    {
      "turn": 6,
      "doctor_question": "Do you often find yourself worrying a lot, even when there isn’t an immediate problem?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Worrying? No, not really. I mean, I don't sit around stressing about things unless there's an actual reason to. Like, if a deadline is coming up, sure, but I don't just worry out of nowhere. I actually have a friend, Sarah, who worries about literally everything. We were supposed to go on a road trip last summer to see this indie band play at a festival in the desert. It was going to be so fun, we rented this vintage VW camper van—which, let me tell you, looks amazing on Instagram but drives like a lawnmower. The clutch was so stiff my leg was sore for three days. Anyway, Sarah spent the whole drive panicking that we were going to run out of gas, even though I had the GPS pulled up and mapped out all the gas stations. She even brought this huge emergency kit with flares and a thermal blanket. I was like, Sarah, we're driving through California, not the Arctic tundra! We did end up getting a flat tire, though, right outside of Joshua Tree. Have you ever been to Joshua Tree? The stars out there are just unbelievable. You can see the Milky Way so clearly. I tried to take a picture with my phone, but it just looked like a black square with a couple of white dots. I really need to get a proper DSLR camera if I'm going to keep going on these trips. But yeah, I'm not really a worrier like she is. I just kind of take things as they come.",
